<BaseLayout
  title="Compliance and Audit"
  description="BeaconGuard Verifiable Decision Records support investigation, reconstruction, replay, and verifiable export for governed AI transactions. Not a claim of legal admissibility."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Compliance and Audit</h1>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard is designed to support <strong>auditability, governance review,
      and decision reconstruction</strong> for governed AI transactions. It produces
      <strong>Verifiable Decision Records</strong> from deterministic ALLOW or DENY
      results at Request Admission and Result-to-Action authorization points.
    </p>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard does not make legal compliance guarantees, does not claim legal
      admissibility of evidence, and does not replace customer privacy, security,
      clinical, AML, fraud, SIEM, IAM, or GRC programs.
    </p>

    <EvidenceLifecycle />

    <p>
      Retention, append-only behavior, durability, and storage integrity depend
      on the configured evidence provider and deployment controls. Investigation
      capabilities in BeaconGuard Verify are lab/prevalidation qualified unless a
      specific deployment states otherwise.
    </p>

    <h2>Compliance Support Objectives</h2>

    <p>BeaconGuard is designed to support common review needs:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Deterministic Decision Results: <strong>ALLOW</strong> or <strong>DENY</strong> at configured authorization points</li>
      <li>Complete transaction identifiers linking related request and action decisions</li>
      <li>Release identity, playbook identity, integration context, and intended-action context</li>
      <li>Explicit reason codes (for example <code>APPROVAL_REQUIRED</code> when approval is absent)</li>
      <li>Signed request metadata, workflow identity, trust signals, freshness windows, and replay-related inputs where configured</li>
      <li>Integrity-protected decision evidence designed for investigation replay and verification</li>
      <li>Separation of Govern (playbook lifecycle) from Authorize (runtime) and Verify (read-only)</li>
      <li>Tenant and environment identity in authorization context where configured</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Complete Transaction Identifiers</h2>

    <p>
      Investigation of a governed AI transaction relies on related decision and
      evidence identifiers that connect Request Admission decisions, provider-result
      binding context, and Result-to-Action decisions. Related identifiers support
      investigation; they are not presented as a named distributed correlator
      product.
    </p>

    <h2>Request and Result Decision Relationship</h2>

    <p>
      A request-admission decision records whether provider invocation was
      permitted. A result/action decision records whether a declared intended
      enterprise action was permitted. Reviewers should inspect both where both
      exist, and understand that action authorization is independent—approving a
      proposal does not resume or mutate a prior DENY.
    </p>

    <h2>Verifiable Decision Record</h2>

    <p>
      Every evaluation produces a structured Verifiable Decision Record containing,
      at minimum where applicable:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Timestamp and request / correlation identifiers</li>
      <li>Decision outcome (<strong>ALLOW</strong> or <strong>DENY</strong>) and reason codes</li>
      <li><strong>release_identity</strong> and <strong>playbook_identity</strong></li>
      <li>Integration context</li>
      <li>Intended-action context for Result-to-Action decisions</li>
      <li>Normalized decision context</li>
      <li>Trust, Freshness, and Replay Check outcomes where applicable</li>
      <li>Optional review obligations that do <strong>not</strong> change the permission result</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Evidence Provenance and Source Traceability</h2>

    <p>
      Evidence provenance and Source Evidence field traceability support
      reconstruction from canonical artifacts. Integrity status must be reported
      honestly. Missing or unavailable fields are reported as unavailable;
      reconstruction does not invent model reasoning or fabricate missing
      artifacts.
    </p>

    <h2>Reconstruction, Replay, and Export</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Deterministic Reconstruction</strong> — derives the logical decision path from canonical evidence</li>
      <li><strong>Replay Verification</strong> — investigation replay of a recorded decision; distinct from admission replay protection; not cryptographic replay of model inference</li>
      <li><strong>Verifiable Export</strong> — investigation package with integrity checks; keys are not browser-held; lab/prevalidation qualification applies</li>
      <li><strong>Report provenance</strong> — executive and investigation reports must preserve provenance to underlying decision evidence</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Replay quality depends on preserved evidence, release availability, and
      environment configuration.
    </p>

    <h2>AI-Insight Non-Authority</h2>

    <p>
      AI-Assisted Insights are a separate non-authoritative capability. They must
      not appear inside the trusted reconstruction path or the runtime
      authorization path. Insights are not evidence authority.
    </p>

    <h2>Redaction and Tenant Isolation</h2>

    <p>
      Redaction and disclosure controls for investigation views are
      deployment- and configuration-dependent. Tenant isolation controls are
      lab-proven with customer-live residual risk remaining a shared
      responsibility. Absolute multi-tenant guarantees are not claimed.
    </p>

    <h2>Evidence Claims (qualified)</h2>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard treats decision output as <strong>evidence for governance and
      audit support</strong>, with these qualified properties:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Evidence is <strong>integrity-protected decision evidence</strong></li>
      <li>It is <strong>designed for investigation replay and verification</strong></li>
      <li>It <strong>preserves release identity and decision context</strong></li>
      <li>It <strong>supports audit and governance review</strong></li>
      <li>It is designed for <strong>evidence-before-effect ordering</strong> where enforcement requires it</li>
      <li>It <strong>supports append-only retention when configured</strong> with a required evidence provider</li>
      <li>It supports <strong>key-identified evidence verification</strong> where configured</li>
      <li>It supports <strong>tamper detection</strong> and <strong>restart continuity</strong> where the configured evidence provider and deployment controls provide those properties</li>
      <li>Retention, durability, and storage integrity controls are <strong>deployment-dependent</strong> and shared with the customer</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Do not describe evidence as “evidence-grade” in the abstract, and do not
      claim court-admissible or legally admissible exports.
    </p>

    <h2>Failure Visibility</h2>

    <p>Failure conditions are explicitly recorded.</p>

    <p>
      If evaluation fails due to missing artifacts, verification errors, trust or
      freshness failures, or internal faults:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The decision fails closed as <strong>DENY</strong></li>
      <li>The failure category is recorded</li>
      <li>No permissive fallback is allowed</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Scope Boundaries</h2>

    <p>BeaconGuard control assertions apply to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Authorization decisions at Request Admission and Result-to-Action points</li>
      <li>Deterministic Playbook Evaluation under an Active Verified Release</li>
      <li>Verifiable Decision Record generation</li>
      <li>Read-only Verify investigation capabilities</li>
    </ul>

    <p>They do not extend to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Model training data or model internal reasoning</li>
      <li>Semantic correctness of AI-generated content</li>
      <li>Enterprise application behavior beyond respecting ALLOW / DENY</li>
      <li>Replacement of EHR, AML, fraud, SIEM, IAM, GRC, case-management, or workflow systems of record</li>
      <li>Legal compliance certification, attestation status, or legal admissibility</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Intended Reviewers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Compliance and risk teams</li>
      <li>Internal and external auditors</li>
      <li>Security and architecture reviewers</li>
      <li>Governance stakeholders</li>
    </ul>

